[THOUGHT] The proposed diagnosis of IgG4-related cholangitis is plausible given the cholestatic lab pattern, bile duct wall thickening and focal dilatation on ultrasound, and negative AMA — which makes classic PSC less likely (though not excluded, as ~5% of PSC is AMA-negative). However, **critical confirmatory data are missing**:  
- Serum IgG4 level has not been reported (required for diagnosis; >2× ULN is highly suggestive, though tissue biopsy remains gold standard);  
- MRCP findings — essential to assess for characteristic features of IgG4-RC (e.g., long-segment, concentric, smooth bile duct strictures, often with pancreatic involvement or hilar lymphadenopathy) — are absent;  
- CA 19-9 is pending; elevated levels would raise concern for malignancy and reduce confidence in an IgG4-RC diagnosis;  
- No histopathologic evidence (e.g., from ERCP brushings, biopsy, or resection), and no assessment of other organ involvement (e.g., pancreas, salivary glands, retroperitoneum) typical of IgG4-RD.  
Given this is Turn 15/16 — one turn remaining — the diagnosis cannot be confirmed without at least serum IgG4 and MRCP results. While clinical suspicion may be high, current evidence is insufficient for definitive diagnosis per established criteria (e.g., HISORt or Comprehensive Diagnostic Criteria for IgG4-RD).  

[DECISION] INCOMPLETE  

[FEEDBACK] Serum IgG4 level and MRCP imaging results are mandatory to support or exclude IgG4-related cholangitis. Additionally, CA 19-9 must be obtained to assess for malignancy. Without these, the diagnosis remains unconfirmed.

